From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ahmedawachi <121810925+ahmedawachi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 28 Sep 2026 10:14:48 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 7/9] networking: close only the client when its buffer cannot
 be allocated

What: new config oom-soft-client-buffers (yes|no, MODIFIABLE). Default
yes on Cygwin/MSYS2 builds (__CYGWIN__), no elsewhere. When it is on,
the client buffers below are allocated with the existing try-allocators
(ztrymalloc_usable, sdstrynewlen, and new sdsTryMakeRoomFor /
sdsTryMakeRoomForNonGreedy), for real socket clients only. A failure
closes that one client instead of aborting the server:

- Query buffer growth in readQueryFromClient() (both the big-argument,
  non-greedy path and the greedy path). Nothing of the request has run,
  so the client gets "-OOM could not allocate the query buffer for this
  request, closing the connection" and is closed after that reply. This
  goes through a new read error, CLIENT_READ_QUERYBUF_OOM, so it works
  from IO threads too. The unread request keeps the socket readable, so
  if the growth fails again before that reply is written (the client is
  not reading earlier replies), the client is closed at once instead of
  being polled forever.
- The size hint in processMultibulkBuffer() and the speculative
  pre-allocation after a fat argument are only optimisations: when they
  fail softly they are skipped (the hint) or fall back to the normal
  16 KB buffer (the pre-allocation), and the read path above decides.
- Reply list nodes created by _addReplyPayloadToList() for plain
  payloads on the client's own reply list. The command has already run,
  so its reply is lost, the same as a network drop: the client is closed
  asynchronously (freeClientAsync / CLIENT_CLOSE_ASAP), and nothing else
  it pipelined runs. For a write command the write stays applied and
  propagated.

Both count in the new INFO stats field client_oom_disconnections (reset
by CONFIG RESETSTAT) and log one WARNING line with the client info, at
most once per second.

Not affected, and still fatal: masters, replicas and monitors, fake
clients (Lua and function callers, modules, AOF loading), copy-avoidance
reply nodes (they hold an object reference), push messages queued during
a command, the keyspace, replication, AOF/RDB, module, Lua and MULTI
allocations, and every other allocation.

Fault injection: DEBUG SET-ALLOC-FAIL-THRESHOLD <bytes> makes the soft
allocations above fail when they request <bytes> or more (0 = off). It
only affects those allocations, only while oom-soft-client-buffers is
on, and DEBUG stays behind enable-debug-command as usual. It works on
every platform, so the behaviour is tested on Linux and macOS too.

Why: in a production deployment of the MSYS2 build, one 1.87 MB reply
allocation failed and the whole cache went down for 33 minutes. With this patch the same failure closes one
connection, which StackExchange.Redis reconnects. Large fresh commits
are the allocations most likely to fail first on this platform. This
buys time; it does not make the server immune: any other allocation
that fails still aborts.

A failed sdsTryMakeRoomFor() leaves 's' valid: sizes of SIZE_MAX/2 or
more, for which ztryrealloc frees the old buffer, are refused before
anything is allocated.

Tests: tests/unit/win-soft-oom.tcl. With the threshold set, a 2 MB
HMGET closes only that client while another client's PING works and
client_oom_disconnections is 1; pipelined commands after it do not run;
a write inside EXEC stays applied; EVAL returning a 2 MB value closes the
caller deterministically; a 2 MB HSET gets -OOM and writes nothing, on a
raw socket and through a regular client; a client with 40 MB of unread
replies whose HSET fails is closed and the event loop stays idle; the
same with io-threads 2; small requests are unaffected; with
oom-soft-client-buffers no the threshold has no effect; the default is
yes only on Cygwin/MSYS2; DEBUG is refused with enable-debug-command no.

Risk: medium. It touches the reply hot path (one extra flag test per new
reply node) and the query buffer growth path. With the option off, the
code paths are the upstream ones (apart from the node cap in the
previous patch).
